nderstanding\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Communication Proficiency\",\"question_type\":\"multiple choice\",\"school_type\":\"International\",\"subject\":\"Science\",\"year\":\"Year 12\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"According to the Law of Reflection, the angle of incidence is equal to the angle of reflection. If the angle of incidence is \\\\(30^\\\\circ\\\\), then the angle of reflection is also \\\\(30^\\\\circ\\\\). The angle between the incident ray and the reflected ray is the sum of the angle of incidence and the angle of reflection: \\\\(30^\\\\circ + 30^\\\\circ = 60^\\\\circ\\\\).\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1476,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-05-03\",\"question_text\":\"According to Snell's Law, when a ray of light passes from an optically denser medium (refractive index \\\\(n_1\\\\)) into an optically rarer medium (refractive index \\\\(n_2\\\\)), such that \\\\(n_1 > n_2\\\\), how does the angle of incidence \\\\(\\\\theta_1\\\\) relate to the angle of refraction \\\\(\\\\theta_2\\\\)?\",\"choice_1\":\"\\\\(\\\\theta_1 > \\\\theta_2\\\\)\",\"choice_2\":\"\\\\(\\\\theta_1 < \\\\theta_2\\\\)\",\"choice_3\":\"\\\\(\\\\theta_1 = \\\\theta_2\\\\)\",\"choice_4\":\"The relationship depends on the wavelength of light.\",\"correct_option\":\"B\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Medium\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Understanding\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Communication Proficiency\",\"question_type\":\"multiple choice\",\"school_type\":\"International\",\"subject\":\"Science\",\"year\":\"Year 12\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"Snell's Law is given by the equation \\\\(n_1 \\\\sin(\\\\theta_1) = n_2 \\\\sin(\\\\theta_2)\\\\). In this scenario, light is passing from an optically denser medium (higher refractive index) to an optically rarer medium (lower refractive index), which means \\\\(n_1 > n_2\\\\). To maintain the equality in Snell's Law, if \\\\(n_1\\\\) is greater than \\\\(n_2\\\\), then \\\\(\\\\sin(\\\\theta_1)\\\\) must be less than \\\\(\\\\sin(\\\\theta_2)\\\\). For angles between 0 and 90 degrees, a smaller sine value corresponds to a smaller angle. Therefore, \\\\(\\\\theta_1 < \\\\theta_2\\\\), indicating that the light ray bends away from the normal.\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1475,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-05-03\",\"question_text\":\"Which of the following statements accurately describe principles derived from Newton's Laws of Motion?\",\"choice_1\":\"An object maintaining a constant velocity implies that the net force acting on it is zero.\",\"choice_2\":\"The acceleration of an object is always in the same direction as the net force acting upon it.\",\"choice_3\":\"For every action force, there is an equal and opposite reaction force that acts on the same object.\",\"choice_4\":\"The inertia of an object is directly proportional to the net force applied to it.\",\"correct_option\":\"AB\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Medium\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Applying\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Technical Competence\",\"question_type\":\"multiple_answer\",\"school_type\":\"International\",\"subject\":\"Physics\",\"year\":\"Year 12\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"Let's analyze each statement:\\n\\nA. According to Newton's First Law of Motion, an object at rest stays at rest and an object in motion stays in motion with the same speed and in the same direction unless acted upon by an unbalanced force. This means if an object is moving with constant velocity (zero acceleration), the net force acting on it must be zero. So, statement A is correct.\\n\\nB. Newton's Second Law of Motion states that the acceleration of an object is directly proportional to the net force acting on it and inversely proportional to its mass, given by the formula \\\\(F = ma\\\\). The direction of the acceleration is always in the same direction as the net force. So, statement B is correct.\\n\\nC. Newton's Third Law of Motion states that for every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction. However, these action-reaction forces act on *different* objects, not the same object. For example, if you push a wall, the wall pushes back on you. Your force acts on the wall, and the wall's force acts on you. So, statement C is incorrect.\\n\\nD. Inertia is a measure of an object's resistance to changes in its state of motion, and it is directly proportional to the object's mass. It is not directly proportional to the net force applied to it. So, statement D is incorrect.\\n\\nTherefore, the correct statements are A and B.\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1474,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-05-03\",\"question_text\":\"Kirchhoff's Current Law (KCL) is a fundamental principle in circuit analysis. This law is based on which of the following conservation principles?\",\"choice_1\":\"A. Conservation of energy\",\"choice_2\":\"B. Conservation of charge\",\"choice_3\":\"C. Conservation of momentum\",\"choice_4\":\"D. Conservation of mass\",\"correct_option\":\"B\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Easy\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Understanding\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Communication Proficiency\",\"question_type\":\"multiple choice\",\"school_type\":\"International\",\"subject\":\"Science\",\"year\":\"Year 12\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"Kirchhoff's Current Law (KCL) states that the algebraic sum of currents entering a node (junction) is equal to the algebraic sum of currents leaving the node. This law is a direct consequence of the principle of conservation of electric charge. Charge cannot be created or destroyed at a junction; therefore, the total charge flowing into the junction must equal the total charge flowing out. This directly implies that the sum of currents entering must equal the sum of currents leaving, reflecting the conservation of charge.\",\"answers_hidden\":false}]}", "url": "http://localhost:6016/index.html", "timestamp": "2026-06-15T17:07:16.122Z"}
