[2026-06-16 17:57:18] {"level": "log", "message": "fetchQuestions: Received response data: {\"total_count\":27,\"questions\":[{\"question_id\":1539,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-06-15\",\"question_text\":\"A spacecraft is moving through deep space at a constant velocity of \\\\(10,000\\\\) m/s relative to a fixed star. If its engines are turned off, what can be inferred about the net external force acting on the spacecraft?\",\"choice_1\":\"The net external force is directed opposite to the spacecraft's motion, causing it to slow down gradually.\",\"choice_2\":\"The net external force is zero, allowing the spacecraft to continue its motion at a constant velocity according to Newton's First Law.\",\"choice_3\":\"The net external force is in the direction of the spacecraft's motion, maintaining its high speed.\",\"choice_4\":\"The net external force is constant and non-zero, as there is still gravitational pull from distant celestial bodies.\",\"correct_option\":\"B\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Medium\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Applying\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Communication Proficiency\",\"question_type\":\"multiple choice\",\"school_type\":\"International\",\"subject\":\"Science\",\"year\":\"Year 12\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"According to Newton's First Law of Motion, an object in motion will stay in motion with the same speed and in the same direction unless acted upon by an unbalanced external force. Since the spacecraft is moving at a constant velocity (meaning zero acceleration) and its engines are off (implying no thrust), the net external force acting on it must be zero. Even in deep space, there might be minuscule gravitational forces, but for practical applications and the context of 'constant velocity' without engines, the net force is considered negligible or zero, allowing the constant velocity to persist. Therefore, option B is the correct inference.\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1538,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-06-15\",\"question_text\":\"The integral equation \\\\(y(x) = f(x) + \\\\lambda \\\\int_{a}^{b} K(x,t) y(t) dt\\\\), where \\\\(a\\\\) and \\\\(b\\\\) are constants, is classified as a Volterra integral equation of the second kind.\",\"choice_1\":\"Accept\",\"choice_2\":\"Reject\",\"choice_3\":null,\"choice_4\":null,\"correct_option\":\"B\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Medium\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Evaluating\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Technical  Competence\",\"question_type\":\"accept_reject\",\"school_type\":\"International\",\"subject\":\"Mathmatics\",\"year\":\"Year 12\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"1. An integral equation of the form \\\\(y(x) = f(x) + \\\\lambda \\\\int_{a}^{b} K(x,t) y(t) dt\\\\), where the upper limit of integration \\\\(b\\\\) is a constant, is known as a Fredholm integral equation of the second kind.\\n2. A Volterra integral equation of the second kind has the form \\\\(y(x) = f(x) + \\\\lambda \\\\int_{a}^{x} K(x,t) y(t) dt\\\\), where the upper limit of integration is the variable \\\\(x\\\\).\\n3. Since the given equation has a constant upper limit \\\\(b\\\\), it is a Fredholm integral equation, not a Volterra integral equation. Therefore, the statement incorrectly classifies the equation and should be rejected.\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1537,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-06-15\",\"question_text\":\"Consider the integral equation \\\\(y(x) = 1 + \\\\int_{0}^{x} y(t) dt\\\\). Is the function \\\\(y(x) = e^x\\\\) a solution to this equation?\",\"choice_1\":\"Accept\",\"choice_2\":\"Reject\",\"choice_3\":null,\"choice_4\":null,\"correct_option\":\"A\",\"difficulty_level\":\"Medium\",\"cognitive_level\":\"Evaluating\",\"learning_outcome\":\"Technical  Competence\",\"question_type\":\"accept_reject\",\"school_type\":\"International\",\"subject\":\"Mathmatics\",\"year\":\"Year 12\",\"major\":null,\"course\":null,\"material\":null,\"semester\":null,\"company\":null,\"department\":null,\"job_role\":null,\"audience_type\":\"school\",\"mark\":5,\"time_seconds\":120,\"discriminating_factor\":null,\"status\":\"pending\",\"variables\":null,\"solution\":\"To verify if \\\\(y(x) = e^x\\\\) is a solution, we substitute it into the integral equation:\\n1. Substitute \\\\(y(x) = e^x\\\\) into the integral equation: \\\\(e^x = 1 + \\\\int_{0}^{x} e^t dt\\\\).\\n2. Evaluate the definite integral: \\\\(\\\\int_{0}^{x} e^t dt = [e^t]_{0}^{x} = e^x - e^0 = e^x - 1\\\\).\\n3. Substitute the result of the integral back into the equation: \\\\(e^x = 1 + (e^x - 1)\\\\).\\n4. Simplify the right side: \\\\(e^x = 1 + e^x - 1\\\\), which simplifies to \\\\(e^x = e^x\\\\).\\nSince the equality holds true for all \\\\(x\\\\), the function \\\\(y(x) = e^x\\\\) is indeed a solution to the given integral equation. Therefore, the statement should be accepted.\",\"answers_hidden\":false},{\"question_id\":1521,\"author_creator\":\"TDM-AI-Engin\",\"date_created\":\"2026-05-15\",\"question_text\":\"\u0647\u0644 \u064a\u0646\u0635 \u0642\u0627\u0646\u0648\u0646 \u0627\u0644\u062c\u0627\u0630\u0628\u064a\u0629 \u0639\u0644\u0649 \u0623\u0646 \u0643\u0644 \u062c\u0633\u0645\u064a\u0646 \u0641\u064a \u0627\u0644\u0643\u0648\u0646 \u064a\u062c\u0630\u0628\u0627\u0646 \u0628\u0639\u0636\u0647\u0645\u0627 \u0627\u0644\u0628\u0639\u0636 \u0628\u0642\u0648\u0629\u061f\",\"choice_1\":\"\u0646\u0639\u0645\",\"choice_2\":\"\u0644\u0627\",\"choice_3\":null,\"choice_4\":null,\"correct_option\":\"A\",\"d